✅ What is JPA?
JPA (Java Persistence API) is a standard specification in Java that defines how Java objects (entities) should be stored and retrieved from a relational database.

➡️ JPA is not a framework, it's just a set of interfaces and annotations.
➡️ You need an implementation of JPA to use it — like Hibernate, EclipseLink, etc.

🔸 JPA vs Hibernate:
| Feature  | JPA                     | Hibernate             |
| -------- | ----------------------- | --------------------- |
| Type     | Specification/API       | Implementation of JPA |
| Use      | Common interface        | Actual ORM framework  |
| Provider | Requires implementation | Is an implementation  |


🔧 Why JPA?
You deal with Java objects, not SQL directly.

It abstracts JDBC boilerplate code.

Easy to map relationships (@OneToMany, etc.).

Integrated well with Spring Boot (via Spring Data JPA).

✅ Key JPA Annotations
| Annotation        | Purpose                            |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `@Entity`         | Marks a class as a DB table        |
| `@Id`             | Marks the primary key              |
| `@GeneratedValue` | Auto-generates PK                  |
| `@Column`         | Maps field to DB column (optional) |
| `@Table`          | Set table name                     |
| `@OneToMany`      | One-to-many relationship           |
| `@ManyToOne`      | Many-to-one relationship           |
| `@JoinColumn`     | Defines FK column                  |


